        // We need to count the number of (da, db, dc) with s <= l and da + db - dc <= z - x - y
        // This is equivalent to counting the number of (da, db, dc) with s <= l and dc >= da + db - (z - x - y)
        // But since dc >= 0, we have dc >= max(0, da + db - (z - x - y))
        // This is complex, so we use a different approach:
        // For each possible total increment t (0 <= t <= l), count the number of (da, db, dc) with da + db + dc = t
        // and (x + da) + (y + db) <= (z + dc)
        // => da + db - dc <= z - x - y
        // Let k = z - x - y (if k < 0, then no invalid cases for this configuration)
        // We need da + db - dc <= k
